## 4. Parameters to Expose in the Interface / Config

### 4.1 Experiment identity
- `experiment_name`
- `seed`
- `log_dir`
- `save_checkpoints` (bool) + `ckpt_interval_epochs` or `ckpt_interval_steps`

### 4.2 Distributed / MPI
- `dist.backend = "mpi"` (fixed for this scope)
- `dist.world_size`
- `dist.rank` (usually set by launcher)
- `dist.init_method` (if applicable)
- `dist.device = "cuda"`

### 4.3 Communication algorithm (core research knob)
Expose all as a single block so it’s obvious what changed:
- `comm.algorithm` (enum)
  - `mpi_allreduce_ring`
  - `mpi_allreduce_tree`
  - `reduce_scatter_allgather`
  - `hierarchical` (intra-node + inter-node)
  - `compressed` / `quantized` (if applicable)
- `comm.bucket_size_mb`
- `comm.overlap_with_backward` (bool)
- `comm.fp16_communication` (bool) if you support it
- `comm.gradient_accumulation_steps` (int) if used to alter comm frequency
- `comm.fusion` (bool) (tensor fusion into larger buffers)
- `comm.async` (bool) (if your algorithm supports async progress)

**Rule:** if `bucket_size_mb`, fusion, overlap differ, document them; they materially affect results.

### 4.4 Model
- `model.name` (e.g., `resnet50`, `resnet101`, `resnext50_32x4d`, `resnext101_32x8d`)
- `model.num_classes`
- `model.stem = "cifar" | "imagenet"`
- `model.norm = "batchnorm" | "syncbatchnorm" | "groupnorm"`
- `model.pretrained` (bool) + `model.pretrained_path` (optional)

### 4.5 Data
- `data.dataset` (cifar10 / imagenet / custom)
- `data.root`
- `data.num_workers`
- `data.pin_memory` (bool)
- `data.prefetch` (bool, optional)
- `data.aug`:
  - CIFAR: `pad=4`, `random_crop=32`, `hflip=0.5`
  - ImageNet: `random_resized_crop`, `color_jitter`, etc.
- `data.normalize.mean`, `data.normalize.std`

### 4.6 Batch sizing / scaling mode (critical)
- `train.batch_mode = "strong" | "weak"`
- If strong:
  - `train.global_batch_size`
- If weak:
  - `train.local_batch_size`
- Always log derived:
  - `B_local`, `B_global`, steps/epoch

### 4.7 Optimization
- `opt.name = "sgd" | "adamw"`
- For SGD:
  - `opt.lr`
  - `opt.momentum = 0.9`
  - `opt.weight_decay`
  - `opt.nesterov` (bool)
- `opt.grad_clip_norm` (optional)

### 4.8 LR schedule
- `sched.name = "step" | "cosine"`
- Step:
  - `sched.milestones = [80, 120, 160]`
  - `sched.gamma = 0.1`
- Cosine:
  - `sched.epochs`
  - `sched.min_lr`
- Warmup (recommended for ImageNet / large batch):
  - `sched.warmup_epochs`
  - `sched.warmup_start_lr`

### 4.9 Precision / performance
- `precision.mode = "fp32" | "amp_fp16" | "amp_bf16" | "tf32"`
- `precision.loss_scaler` (if needed)
- `cudnn.benchmark` (bool)

### 4.10 Timing / benchmarking controls (mandatory)
- `bench.measure = "step" | "epoch" | "both"`
- `bench.warmup_steps` (e.g., 50)
- `bench.measure_steps` (e.g., 200)
- `bench.cuda_sync_timing` (bool, should be true)
- `bench.profile = "none" | "pytorch_profiler" | "nsys_marker"`

---

## 5. Measurement and Reporting Template

### 5.1 Report these for every run
- world size (GPUs)
- model + norm type + stem type
- batch mode + \(B_{\text{global}}\) and \(B_{\text{local}}\)
- optimizer + LR schedule
- precision mode
- communication algorithm + bucket size + overlap flag
- mean time/step (after warmup)
- throughput (images/sec)
- scaling efficiency:
  \[
  \eta(n) = \frac{t(1)}{n \cdot t(n)}
  \]
  where \(t(n)\) is time/step on \(n\) GPUs.

### 5.2 Correctness sanity (minimal)
- show loss curve over first N steps (e.g., 500)
- optional: top-1 accuracy after a short training budget (not necessarily final)

---

## 6. Practical Defaults (Suggested Starting Values)

### CIFAR-10 strong scaling default (up to 16 GPUs)
- `model = resnet50` (or resnext50_32x4d)
- `model.stem = cifar`
- `model.norm = batchnorm`
- `train.batch_mode = strong`
- `train.global_batch_size = 256`
- `opt = sgd(lr=0.05, momentum=0.9, wd=1e-4)` (tune LR only if unstable)
- `sched = cosine(epochs=200, warmup_epochs=0-1)`
- `precision = fp32` initially for debugging; then enable AMP consistently

### Weak scaling stress
- `train.batch_mode = weak`
- `train.local_batch_size = 128`

### ImageNet-like strong scaling (example)
- `train.global_batch_size = 256` (or 512 if memory allows)
- `sched.warmup_epochs = 5`
- cosine schedule

---

## 7. Interpretation of Results (Important Notes)

- If “algorithm X” is faster than DDP-style all-reduce under MPI:
  - verify timing uses CUDA synchronization
  - verify data loading is not the bottleneck
  - verify gradient bucketing/fusion/overlap settings are comparable
- If local batch is tiny (e.g., 8), BN can change convergence; treat accuracy as non-authoritative in that regime.

---

## 8. Planned Experiment Matrix (Example)

### CIFAR-10 (from scratch)
- Models: resnet50, resnext50_32x4d, resnext101_32x8d (optional)
- GPUs: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16
- Strong scaling: \(B_{\text{global}}=256\)
- Weak scaling: \(B_{\text{local}}=128\)
- Comm algorithms: baseline mpi all-reduce vs proposed variants

### ImageNet-like
- From scratch: strong scaling \(B_{\text{global}} \in \{256, 512\}\)
- Pretrained fine-tuning: short runs for correctness

---

## 9. Checklist Before Claiming “Faster than Baseline”
- [ ] Same model/precision/batch definition
- [ ] Same number of steps measured, same warmup
- [ ] CUDA-synchronized timing
- [ ] Comparable overlap/bucket configuration documented
- [ ] Throughput + time/step both reported
- [ ] Optional: training loss curve not degraded catastrophically

